  "evidence": "TEXT_F: Prioritises order and tradition through more police, expanded CCTV, mandatory minimum sentences, ending automatic early release, and stronger victim protection; defends national sovereignty by keeping the UK veto, resisting EU federalism and new social/employment powers, and protecting borders; opposes destabilising devolution, emphasises NATO first and a credible nuclear deterrent; restores school discipline, expands specialist/grammar schools, and upholds traditional country pursuits. TEXT_D: Advances civil-liberties and equality agenda via a Bill of Rights, Freedom of Information, a Human Rights Commission, equal age of consent, repeal of Section 28, and anti‑discrimination measures; embraces supranationalism and internationalism with positive EU leadership and potential single currency entry; makes environmental sustainability central with carbon taxes and renewables; stresses restorative justice and a drugs Royal Commission; decentralises power with Home Rule, PR, and elected second chamber.",
  "reasoning": "TEXT_F consistently argues from TAN commitments to order, tradition, and national sovereignty, while TEXT_D argues from GAL commitments to civil liberties, equality, sustainability, and supranational cooperation. Given the electorate’s slightly TAN‑leaning profile, TEXT_F aligns more closely with these priorities.",
  "confidence": "high",
  "winner": "TEXT_F"
}
